git commit -m </span><span><span class="hljs-string">"Describe your changes"</span></span><span>
git push origin main
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
</li>
<li data-start="539" data-end="676">
<p data-start="542" data-end="676"><strong data-start="542" data-end="553">Netlify</strong> will automatically detect the push to <code data-start="592" data-end="598">main</code>, pull the changes, and rebuild/deploy your site—no extra commands required.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="678" data-end="836">You can follow the progress or roll back from the <strong data-start="728" data-end="739">Deploys</strong> tab of your site dashboard:<br data-start="767" data-end="770">
<a data-start="770" data-end="834" rel="noopener" target="_new" class="" href="https://app.netlify.com/projects/same-k9b4q7zbb4s-latest/deploys">https://app.netlify.com/projects/same-k9b4q7zbb4s-latest/deploys</a></p>
<hr data-start="838" data-end="841">
<h3 data-start="843" data-end="893">(Optional) Preview Deploys for Pull Requests</h3>
<p data-start="894" data-end="969">If you’d like Netlify to spin up draft URLs for each GitHub Pull Request:</p>
<ol data-start="971" data-end="1144">
<li data-start="971" data-end="1053">
<p data-start="974" data-end="1053">Go to <strong data-start="980" data-end="1032">Site settings → Build &amp; deploy → Deploy contexts</strong> in the Netlify UI.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1054" data-end="1144">
<p data-start="1057" data-end="1144">Under <strong data-start="1063" data-end="1082">Deploy previews</strong>, ensure <strong data-start="1091" data-end="1130">“Automatically build pull requests”</strong> is enabled.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="1146" data-end="1231">Now each new PR will get its own preview URL—great for QA before merging to <code data-start="1222" data-end="1228">main</code>.</p>
<hr data-start="1233" data-end="1236">
<p data-start="1238" data-end="1417">That’s it! From here on out, <strong data-start="1267" data-end="1285">every git push</strong> to your <code data-start="1294" data-end="1300">main</code> branch equals a fresh deploy, hands‑off. Let me know if you’d like to add any further CI checks or preview features!</p>
